dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/logs/codex-review.out:20:Context: the goal is to visually 'elevate' a dark statusline-configurator web UI. The approach builds a reference-sourcing pipeline: a skills-first brief (4 paid LLM agents, regenerated every run) -> a browser agent that searches Dribbble (human-cadence, collections, hex filters) -> a unanimous 4-skill convergence (4 more paid LLM agents) -> operator gates -> then an elevation loop. Every automated reference run so far has FAILED (first pool was logos/junk; second was internally inconsistent and hit a bot-check).
dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/logs/codex-review.out:22:Critique specifically: (a) PROPORTIONALITY - is this ~8-paid-agent-per-run pipeline justified to produce ~5 reference images that are merely an INPUT to the real UI work, vs a far leaner alternative? (b) FAILURE MODES - what is most likely to break or produce bad output? (c) UNPROVEN ASSUMPTIONS specced as settled (e.g. a subagent driving a shared visible browser; collections needing login + being write-actions on the user's account; bot-check recurring despite 'human cadence'). (d) MISSING REQUIREMENTS or CONTRADICTIONS. (e) What would you cut or change first? Be concrete, terse, and skeptical. End with a 1-paragraph bottom-line verdict.
