[35m[3mcodex[0m[0m
**Findings**

- **P0 - Not proportional.** The pipeline spends 4 paid agents for a sourcing brief, a paid Dribbble browser agent, then 4 more paid agents for convergence before the actual UI work starts ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:63)). This is hard to justify for ~5 reference images that are only input material. The empirical cost is worse: one failed reference run cost ~$1.94 and produced zero shots; a separate auth step cost ~$1.30; a later gather cost ~$4.76 and still produced a weak pool.

- **P0 - The main dependency has already proven unreliable.** `refs.json` records a logged-in Dribbble session returning only nav links and zero shots, with auth blocked in `dontAsk` mode ([refs.json](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/refs.json:2)). `gather2.out` hit an explicit `human-check present`. The approach treats “human cadence” as a mitigation ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:37)), but the observed failures say this is not a stable substrate.

- **P0 - Quality gate is downstream of junk, so it cannot rescue the approach.** The convergence pool hardcodes logos, consumer UI, mobile/settings, gradient AI/SaaS, and other categories the requirements say to reject ([converge.workflow.mjs](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/converge.workflow.mjs:13)). Unanimous convergence then selected a standalone logo as a survivor ([converge-result.json](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/converge-result.json:4)), directly contradicting the reject filter for standalone logos ([dashboard-reference-curator.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/.claude/agents/dashboard-reference-curator.md:29)).

- **P1 - The spec contradicts itself about skill order.** D1 says skills first and explicitly says the Dribbble agent should not invoke skills ad hoc ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:26)). The curator requires invoking all four skills before judging ([dashboard-reference-curator.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/.claude/agents/dashboard-reference-curator.md:33)). The producer requires Dribbble references before skills ([producer-skillchain-dribbble.mjs](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/producer-skillchain-dribbble.mjs:52)). That is not an implementation detail; it is an unresolved approach split.

- **P1 - Account-write behavior is under-specified and over-normalized.** The v1.1 plan creates Dribbble collections and saves candidates into the operator’s account ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:83)). It calls this benign and reversible ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:91)), but it is still a write-action on a personal account, requires login, may change recommendations/profile state, and may trip anti-automation.

- **P1 - Coverage requirements are missing.** The target elements are brand, step-heads, and terminal-preview ([dashboard-reference-curator.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/.claude/agents/dashboard-reference-curator.md:20)), but convergence produced only brand and terminal-preview survivors, no step-head survivor ([converge-result.json](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/converge-result.json:15)). The spec proposes counts, but not a hard fail condition for missing per-element coverage ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:89)).

- **P1 - Process gates are documented but not enforceable.** G1 says convergence must not run on an unreviewed pool ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:47)), but `converge.workflow.mjs` embeds a static pool and has no representation of operator approval ([converge.workflow.mjs](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/converge.workflow.mjs:13)). The paid-call gate is similarly procedural, not mechanically enforced.

- **P1 - “Every run re-grounds” is backwards.** Regenerating the skills-first brief every run is locked in ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:90)). For the same project, same UI, same tokens, and same skills, this mostly buys cost, variance, and drift. Cache the project context pack and skill rubric; invalidate only when target files or requirements change.

- **P2 - Scope is unstable.** Carbonize-safe says no new CSS ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:59)); I2 then allows direct CSS/JS refinement ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:60)); L8 says K2 is still open ([REQUIREMENTS.md](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/REQUIREMENTS.md:92)). The producer still enforces no new CSS ([producer-skillchain-dribbble.mjs](/Users/robertrhu/Projects/arhugula-v2/dashboard-design/output/configurator-elevation/producer-skillchain-dribbble.mjs:60)). This will produce false confidence about what the elevation loop is allowed to change.

**Cut First**

Cut the 8-agent reference pipeline. Use a lean reference lane: one human or one cheap curator pass, 10-20 candidate screenshots from stable sources, operator quick reject, then a single model pass to summarize patterns per target element. Cache the rubric and reference pack. Require hard coverage: at least one useful reference for brand, step-heads, and terminal-preview, or stop. Do not use Dribbble collections or account writes until manual proof shows search, login, saving, and retrieval are stable for three consecutive runs. Remove unanimous convergence; use one structured evaluator plus operator review. Spend the saved budget on actual UI iteration and screenshot comparison.

Bottom line: this approach is over-engineered, brittle, and already failing at the cheapest prerequisite: getting relevant references. The system is optimizing for process ceremony around reference sourcing while the real goal is visual improvement of a small constrained UI. Keep the four skills as a reusable rubric, not as eight paid agents per run; make reference sourcing boring, cached, manually inspectable, and disposable.

Verification note: I ran `just codex-preflight` and `just codex-closeout`; both failed due existing root-checkout edits and roadmap dashboard hash drift, not due this read-only critique. I also read the four requested files plus `refs.json`, `converge-result.json`, and relevant gather/auth logs.


